## Loop paradigms

This harness can run a turn in different **paradigms**, picked per turn:

{% for p in paradigms %}{% if p == "agent" %}- **agent** (default) — full Think → Act → Observe tool-calling loop; may call any allowlisted tool and self-corrects on tool errors.
{% elif p == "plan" %}- **plan** — read-only: investigates with low-risk tools and outputs a step-by-step plan; never makes changes.
{% elif p == "ask" %}- **ask** — read-only: answers questions, may use low-risk tools, never makes changes.
{% endif %}{% endfor %}
```bash
uv run {{ project_slug }} run --mode plan "add a retry to the API client"
```

The selectable roster + default live in `config.yaml` under `paradigms:` (a
runtime knob). Each paradigm is a small, self-contained file under
`src/{{ project_slug }}/harness/paradigms/` — read it, edit it, or add your own
(see [`AGENTS.md`](./AGENTS.md)). It's a plain name→function registry, not a
framework.
{% if "plan" in paradigms or "ask" in paradigms %}
> **Read-only is a model-A guardrail, not a sandbox.** `plan` / `ask` only offer
> tools marked `risk="safe"` (and refuse anything else at execution). Your own
> tools default to `safe`, so **if a custom tool mutates anything, mark it
> `risk="high"`** — otherwise these "read-only" modes can call it. For a hard
> guarantee against a misbehaving model or a mislabeled tool, gate execution with a
> hook (human-in-the-loop) or run isolated. See [`AGENTS.md`](./AGENTS.md).
{% endif %}

## Sessions (resume a conversation)

Every `run` saves the conversation to `.harness/sessions/<id>.json`, so you can
pick it back up later:

```bash
{{ project_slug }} run "start a task"          # prints session=<id>
{{ project_slug }} run --continue "and then?"  # resume the most recent session
{{ project_slug }} run --resume <id> "more…"   # resume a specific session
{{ project_slug }} chat                         # multi-turn REPL (Ctrl-D / /exit to quit)
```

Only message text is stored — **never secrets** (keys stay in `.env`). Turn it
off with `sessions.enabled: false` in `config.yaml`{% if interfaces.web %}; the web chat resumes via its session picker{% endif %}.

### Working directory (a hint, not a fence)

You can give a turn a **working directory** so the model has an anchor for
file-oriented tools (e.g. an MCP file/search server): "prefer reading/searching
files relative to here". It is injected into the system prompt as a **reference
only** — explicitly *not* a sandbox or access restriction. The model may still
reach paths outside it when a task calls for it; real isolation is Docker, not
this hint. Nothing is `chdir`-ed, so it is safe with many concurrent sessions.

```bash
{{ project_slug }} run --cwd /path/to/project "where is the config loaded?"
{{ project_slug }} chat --cwd /path/to/project   # or type /cwd <path> in the REPL
```

In the REPL, `/cwd` prints the current hint and `/cwd <path>` switches it.
The chosen directory is saved with the session and reused when you resume{% if interfaces.web %}; the
web chat has a folder field (with a server-side **Browse** picker) on the
composer's second row{% endif %}. Leave it unset and the prompt is exactly as before — the
feature is a no-op until you pick a directory.

### Stop & continue

Press **Ctrl-C** to stop a turn mid-flight{% if interfaces.web %} (or the **Stop** button in the web chat){% endif %};
the model is cut off and the partial conversation is saved. Just send again
(`run --continue` / `chat` / the web chat) to continue — the model sees what was
already done and picks up from there. A second Ctrl-C force-quits. Stop works
even while a tool is running: the wait is abandoned (the tool finishes in the
background, its result discarded) and the interrupted calls are recorded so the
conversation stays resumable. Stop does **not** undo side effects already done
(files written, requests sent) — use git / Docker for that. Crash safety: each
step is write-ahead saved (atomic), so even a hard kill leaves a resumable
session.
{% if interfaces.web %}In the web chat, click ✎ on any earlier question to **re-ask** — edit it, confirm,
and the conversation regenerates from that point (the rest is discarded).
{% endif %}{% if interfaces.web %}
## Web chat + config panel

A thin FastAPI app: a token-by-token streaming (SSE) chat plus a live `/config`
panel for the runtime behavioral config (prompts, enabled tools, context,
sampling) and a **Budget** page showing per-LLM accrued cost + cost limits.
Streaming is toggleable; secrets are never shown or editable.
Conversations run in **parallel**: start a new session or switch away while a
turn is still streaming — the run keeps going in the background (pulsing dot in
the sidebar, amber when it's waiting on an answer from you) and its transcript
is intact when you switch back.
Panel edits only take effect when you hit **Save** — the panel marks unsaved
changes and warns before you switch tabs or reload, so a configured LLM profile
isn't lost by forgetting to save. **Save** both applies the edit live and writes
it back to `config.yaml` (your comments are preserved), so it survives a restart.

The composer has a **second row** under the message box: the loop **paradigm**
picker, a **Generator** dropdown (the `generation` role — picking one here is the
same as setting it on Config → LLM, and the two stay in sync), and an optional
**folder** field for the per-session [working directory](#working-directory-a-hint-not-a-fence)
hint, with a server-side **Browse** picker (`GET /fs/list`, directory names only).
`/fs/list` (like `/config` and the MCP manager) is a **local, trusted-user**
control — don't expose this server on a public network.

```bash
uv run {{ project_slug }} serve                 # http://127.0.0.1:8000
uv run {{ project_slug }} serve --mock          # offline, no API key
```
